I've started playing again recently (ascended W and M, though didn't make
YAAP), and naturally I've come up with a few ideas for the game. I'm
capable of coding them myself, but not inclined to do so just yet (I'd
prefer to get every class in the game at least through their quest before I
go changing the game). Many of my ideas concern cursed items, because I
think the current effects of cursing are weak ("oh darn, my "oLS is
cursed... no matter, didn't plan on taking it off anyway"). So here are the
ideas:

Misc.

* Cursed worn items (rings/amulets/boots/gloves/cloaks) should flicker on
and off (that is, only provide their protection some of the time). This
wouldn't apply to those items designed to be harmful (we'll assume the
designers account for the effects of cursing). Not quite sure what to do
about levitation, though. Consistency would say that it too should flicker
on and off, though that would be an improvement over the current situation.
Not sure if this should apply to dragon scales/DSM or shields of reflection
either (depends on whether or not you view the protection as a property of
the material or as magic). As for balance, I believe this would have wider
implications in the late game than in the beginning (since most beneficial
items aren't generated cursed anyway). Creatures which can curse objects
become more dangerous, and scrolls of remove curse/holy water become more
valuable. Eating a cursed item would confer a flickering intrinsic ("You
feel somewhat..."). The question then would be, what happens if you eat
another cursed item, or if you eat an uncursed/blessed item? Another
possibility would be to confer the intrisic only if it happens to be "on"
at the moment you eat the ring.

* It would be nice if you could get a fuzzy idea of the health of a monster
(particularly steeds). Something like "The <foo> looks healthy." to "The
<foo> seems almost dead." Only if you're not blind, though. Not quite sure
what command to put this on.

* New room type: Music shop. Sells flutes/harps/drums/horns. Might be nice
to have a garunteed one somewhere in the dungeon, so you'd always have some
way to get through the castle.

Artifacts:

* Master Key of Thievery: Invocation should be able to untrap ANY trap
(except magic portals) without any help from outside items (like cans of
grease). This is an artifact, after all. Should be balanced by normal
invocation timeout.

Potions:

* Cursed healing potions should heal only 1/2 what they normally do, or not
at all, or maybe even do a bit of damage (1/4 of what they would have
healed?)

* Damaged organic items can be repaired by dipping in potions of healing.
Amount of repair varies based on type of potion and BUC status. Maybe
organic items could even be fixed by dipping in a blessed potion of full
healing (though who would waste a !oFH on that I don't know).

* Cursed monster detection potions show all monsters as lichen (or myabe
Rodneys)?

Roles:

* Rogues: Add basic skill in enchantment spells, after all, sleep/slow/charm
monster seem like just the kinds of things thieves would have use for.
Maybe lower matter skill to basic (knock is the only real thieving spell in
there, though a certain case could be made for dig).

* Rogue quest: certain changes to prevent it from being impossible in
certain cases (as it nearly was for me). See spoilers below for more
specific information.

* Samurai: Reduce martial arts skill to master, if only to give Monks
something they can do better than anyone else (makes a certain amount of
sense, since samurai do spend some time learning weapons).

Skills:

* Make a way for those of sufficient riding skill (say, skilled?) to
activate the breath weapon of a ridden dragon (are there any other steeds
which have special abilities?). I was thinking of having #monster check
your steed if you don't have a special ability (and if you do, then check
it after using yours).

Wands:

* Cursed wands don't always behave, several possibilities:
        * don't respond to being zapped
        * directional wands fire in random direction
        * act as if they were applied instead of zapped

* Damaged wands sometimes act like cursed wands (probability dependent on
level of damage).

* Both these effects would be balanced by playing with the probabilities.

I'd love to hear your thoughts on these ideas.

